    "product_id": "a61ac10b-9044-44a0-b8aa-e31cbe84fab0",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 040815\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n115 AM MST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Major waterways such as the Salt and Gila rivers will continue to \nexperience high volume flows through at least the end of the week \ndue to dam releases.\n \n- With the exception of a slight chance of mountain showers late \nthis week, dry weather will be common through at least early next \nweek. \n\n- Temperatures a few degrees below normal will warm very close to \nthe daily normals by the end of the week through early next week. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /Today through Friday/... \nEarly morning objective analysis depicts an omega block type flow \npattern across the Conus with negative height anomalies situated \nover the Great Basin and upper Midwest respectively, while pinched \nridging has settled over the northern plains/south-central Canada. A \nstrong jet level u-wind component along the southern tier of the US \nwill ensure this blocking pattern is resolved quickly over the next \n48 hours transitioning into progressive, low amplitude flow pattern \nfeaturing elongated, positively tilted troughs. By the end of the \nweek, there's sufficient ensemble evidence of weakness in the height \nfields over the Southwest resulting in the formation of a cutoff \nlow, albeit with low confidence on the placement and intensity. This \ncould result in a few light showers over mountains of eastern \nArizona Friday, though moisture levels will be severely limited. \n\nRegardless, under local quasi-zonal flow, H5 heights will generally \nhover in a 576-582dm range the next several days with slowly warming \nlower tropospheric temperatures. Given the generally dry pattern, \nforecast confidence is excellent that temperatures around 4F below \nnormal will rebound very close to the seasonal average by the end of \nthe week. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /Saturday through Tuesday/... \nDuring the weekend, some semblance of a Rex block (or omega block) \nwill develop in the southern stream with weak troughing over \nnorthern Mexico and broad ridging across the Great Basin/central \nRockies. While the precise orientation and positioning of salient \nfeatures remain uncertain based on notable ensemble differences, the \noverall pattern will promote surface pressure rises over the \nintermountain West and dry N/NE boundary layer flow. Based on the \npattern, periods of breezy northeast winds are likely at times over \nthe weekend and early next week, though nothing particularly strong \nor unusual for this time of year. At some point early next week, the \nbulk of ensemble members eject the northern Mexico wave through the \nforecast area bringing enhanced mid/upper level moisture. It remains \nto be seen whether this process can overcome the dry boundary layer \nairmass, with the highest odds suggesting dry weather persisting. \nWith H5 heights remaining nearly steady state in a 580-584dm range, \ntemperatures should follow a near persistence trends with readings \nnot terribly far from climatology. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0530Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT; and \nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\n\nNo major aviation weather concerns are expected through the TAF \nperiod under FEW-SCT passing high clouds. For the Phoenix Metro, \neasterly winds will develop during the overnight hours and then go \nback westerly tomorrow afternoon.  Across SE CA, winds at KIPL will \nfavor a W/NW component, while directions favor the NW at KBLH. Wind \nspeeds will be aob 7 kt at all terminals with extended periods of \nvariability to at time calm conditions expected.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nWhile a stray shower may affect mountains of eastern districts \nFriday, the area will remain dry with little to no chance of wetting \nrainfall through early next week. Minimum afternoon humidity levels \nwill largely fall into a 20-30% range in western districts and 30-\n50% in eastern districts. This will follow fair to good overnight \nrecovery in western districts and good to excellent levels in eastern \ndistricts. Winds speeds will remain seasonably light for the \nmajority of the period, albeit obtaining 20-30 mph northerly gusts \nthrough the lower Colorado River valley late in the week. Increased \neasterly winds will also be likely in eastern districts early next \nweek. \n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...18\nLONG TERM...18\nAVIATION...Berislavich\nFIRE WEATHER...18\n",
